    {
      "crop_slug": "sweet-alyssum",
      "field_path": "description_seasoned",
      "our_prose": "Sweet alyssum (Lobularia maritima) is a low, carpet-forming cool-season annual in the mustard family, growing just 3 to 9 inches tall with a wider spread and smothering itself in tiny, honey-scented white, pink, or lavender flowers held in dense clusters. Its signature value is as a premier insectary plant: the sweet-scented blooms draw syrphid (hover) flies, parasitic wasps, ladybugs, and lacewings, whose larvae and broods prey on aphids, which is why growers interplant it through vegetable beds and why it is used in California organic lettuce and broccoli fields as part of an aphid biological-control plan. It blooms best in the cool of spring and fall; growth and flowering slow and the plants look ragged in summer heat, then revive with a light shearing and cooler weather. It self-sows readily.",
